  "question": {
    "text": "For real $x$, let $f(x)=x^3+5 x+1$, then",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "lhnvr",
      "text": "\n$f$ is one-one but not onto $R$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "BVRnP",
      "text": "\n$\\mathrm{f}$ is onto $\\mathrm{R}$ but not one-one\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "vl0Xx",
      "text": "\n$f$ is one-one and onto $R$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "j195b",
      "text": "\n$f$ is neither one-one nor onto $R$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Given $f(x)=x^3+5 x+1$\nNow $f^{\\prime}(x)=3 x^2+5>0, \\forall x \\in R$\n$\\therefore \\mathrm{f}(\\mathrm{x})$ is strictly increasing function\n$\\therefore$ It is one-one\nClearly, $f(x)$ is a continuous function and also increasing on $R$,\n$\\operatorname{Lt}_{x \\rightarrow-\\infty} f(x)=-\\infty$ and $\\operatorname{Lt}_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x)=\\infty$\n$\\therefore \\mathrm{f}(\\mathrm{x})$ takes every value between $-\\infty$ and $\\infty$.\nThus, $f(x)$ is onto function.",
    "image": null
  },
